WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.049
Every security tool watches one place and sees one kind of evidence.

2
00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:11.955
In this demonstration I take three situations at a school and decide which tool would catch each one,

3
00:00:11.955 --> 00:00:16.537
and which second tool would still catch it if the first one failed.

4
00:00:16.537 --> 00:00:19.067
That second tool is defense in depth.

5
00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:25.768
The situations are different from the eight in Activity 4.1, so watch the method, not the answers.

6
00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:32.188
Start from the Layers of Defense table in your Day 4 handout.

7
00:00:32.188 --> 00:00:35.502
I grouped it by where each tool looks.

8
00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:38.991
Anti-virus looks at files on a computer.

9
00:00:38.991 --> 00:00:43.003
EDR watches what programs do on that computer.

10
00:00:43.003 --> 00:00:48.237
Firewalls, IDS and IPS sit where traffic enters the network.

11
00:00:48.237 --> 00:00:50.853
A WAF guards only the website.

12
00:00:50.853 --> 00:00:53.034
DLP watches data leaving.

13
00:00:53.034 --> 00:00:56.435
NDR studies traffic inside the network.

14
00:00:56.435 --> 00:00:59.924
The SIEM collects logs from all of them.

15
00:00:59.924 --> 00:01:06.727
Threat intelligence is a list of known bad addresses, domains and file hashes.

16
00:01:06.727 --> 00:01:10.740
And OT and IoT security guard special devices.

17
00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:12.920
Scenario one.

18
00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:20.151
An attacker on the internet sends a known exploit to the school's mail server, MAIL-01,

19
00:01:20.151 --> 00:01:24.306
and the school wants it stopped before it arrives.

20
00:01:24.306 --> 00:01:26.052
Where does it happen?

21
00:01:26.052 --> 00:01:27.714
At the network edge.

22
00:01:27.714 --> 00:01:28.877
What evidence?

23
00:01:28.877 --> 00:01:32.784
A packet that matches a known attack signature.

24
00:01:32.784 --> 00:01:38.352
Both IDS and IPS can recognize it, but an IDS only raises an alert.

25
00:01:38.352 --> 00:01:41.760
An IPS sits in-line and drops the packet.

26
00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:46.414
Because the school wants it stopped, the primary is IPS.

27
00:01:46.414 --> 00:01:55.639
Second layer: the SIEM, which keeps the IPS log and can connect it with anything that happens on MAIL-01 later.

28
00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:57.811
Scenario two.

29
00:01:57.811 --> 00:02:03.086
Every night from midnight to five, a computer-lab PC, LAB-PC-14,

30
00:02:03.086 --> 00:02:09.020
sends a small message to the same rare outside domain every ten minutes.

31
00:02:09.020 --> 00:02:10.668
No file was flagged.

32
00:02:10.668 --> 00:02:12.399
Where does it happen?

33
00:02:12.399 --> 00:02:13.635
On the network.

34
00:02:13.635 --> 00:02:14.789
What evidence?

35
00:02:14.789 --> 00:02:20.229
A pattern: the same small connection, again and again, on a timer.

36
00:02:20.229 --> 00:02:30.036
That pattern is called beaconing, and it is what NDR is built to notice, because it studies traffic behavior over time.

37
00:02:30.036 --> 00:02:37.372
Second layer: EDR on LAB-PC-14, which can show which program is making those connections.

38
00:02:38.472 --> 00:02:39.626
Scenario three.

39
00:02:39.626 --> 00:02:46.166
An advisory from CERT-PH, the Philippines' national computer emergency response team,

40
00:02:46.166 --> 00:02:49.783
lists a malicious domain, cdn-sync dot example.

41
00:02:49.783 --> 00:02:54.169
Our DNS logs show a faculty PC looked it up this morning.

42
00:02:54.169 --> 00:02:55.785
Where does it happen?

43
00:02:55.785 --> 00:02:58.017
A lookup leaving the network.

44
00:02:58.017 --> 00:02:59.094
What evidence?

45
00:02:59.094 --> 00:03:04.249
A name that is already on a published list of known bad indicators.

46
00:03:04.249 --> 00:03:10.713
That matching job belongs to threat intelligence: the feed is loaded into our tools,

47
00:03:10.713 --> 00:03:13.868
and they compare every lookup against it.

48
00:03:13.868 --> 00:03:20.640
Second layer: the next-generation firewall, using the same feed to block the connection.

49
00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:23.322
Two common mistakes.

50
00:03:23.322 --> 00:03:26.725
First, answering anti-virus for everything.

51
00:03:26.725 --> 00:03:28.782
Anti-virus looks at files.

52
00:03:28.782 --> 00:03:34.163
In scenario two no file was flagged, so anti-virus would stay quiet.

53
00:03:34.163 --> 00:03:37.091
Second, choosing the same tool twice.

54
00:03:37.091 --> 00:03:44.134
The second layer must be a different tool that sees the situation from a different place.

55
00:03:44.134 --> 00:03:51.177
Then if one tool is switched off, out of date, or fooled, the other one still catches it.

56
00:03:51.177 --> 00:03:53.155
That is defense in depth.

57
00:03:54.256 --> 00:03:56.124
Here is my finished table.

58
00:03:56.124 --> 00:04:03.097
For each situation I can say where it happened, what evidence it left, and why that tool sees it.

59
00:04:03.097 --> 00:04:08.632
Be ready to defend your choice the same way: name the place and the evidence.

60
00:04:09.731 --> 00:04:10.483
Your turn.

61
00:04:10.483 --> 00:04:14.538
In the CTM Virtual Lab, choose Day 4 and Activity 4.1.

62
00:04:14.538 --> 00:04:18.368
Discuss the eight bank scenarios with your partner.

63
00:04:18.368 --> 00:04:25.203
For each one ask the same questions: where does it happen, and what evidence does it leave?

64
00:04:25.203 --> 00:04:33.539
Then choose the primary solution and a different second layer in your Lab Notebook, and press Check my answers.

